{"name":"Classic Movie","hex":"#728284","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"classic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to the first class or rank, especially in literature or art.","Exemplary of a particular style; defining a class or category; typical; archetypical; epitomic."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A perfect and/or early example of a particular style.","An artistic work of lasting worth, such as a film or song; a work of enduring excellence."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/classic"},{"term":"movie","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A recorded sequence of images displayed on a screen at a rate sufficiently fast to create the appearance of motion; a film; a video.","A professionally made motion picture of a substantive narrative work, typically around 1 to 3 hours long, typically intended for cinema, art, entertainment, and a widespread audience; a feature film, theatrical film."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/movie"}],"entries":[{"title":"Classic","blocks":[{"text":"A classic is an outstanding example of a particular style; something of lasting worth or with a timeless quality; of the first or highest quality, class, or rank – something that exemplifies its class. The word can be an adjective or a noun. It denotes a particular quality in art, architecture, literature, design, technology, or other cultural artifacts. In commerce, products are named 'classic' to denote a long-standing popular version or model, to distinguish it from a newer variety. Classic is used to describe many major, long-standing sporting events. Colloquially, an everyday occurrence may be described in some dialects of English as 'an absolute classic'."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Classic"},{"title":"Film","blocks":[{"text":"A film, movie, or motion picture is a work of visual art that simulates experiences and otherwise communicates ideas, stories, perceptions, emotions, or atmosphere through the use of moving images that are generally, since the 1930s, synchronized with sound and sometimes feature other sensory stimuli."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Le_Voyage_dans_la_Lune_%281902%29.webm/330px--Le_Voyage_dans_la_Lune_%281902%29.webm.jpg","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Film"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Prof._Stampfer%27s_Stroboscopische_Scheibe_No._X.gif/500px-Prof._Stampfer%27s_Stroboscopische_Scheibe_No._X.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Prof._Stampfer%27s_Stroboscopische_Scheibe_No._X.gif/500px-Prof._Stampfer%27s_Stroboscopische_Scheibe_No._X.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Animated GIF of Prof. Stampfer's Stroboscopische Scheibe No. X (Trentsensky & Vieweg 1833)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AProf._Stampfer's_Stroboscopische_Scheibe_No._X.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/The_Horse_in_Motion-anim.gif/330px-The_Horse_in_Motion-anim.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/The_Horse_in_Motion-anim.gif/330px-The_Horse_in_Motion-anim.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An animation of the retouched Sallie Garner card from The Horse in Motion series (1878–1879) by Muybridge","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Horse_in_Motion-anim.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Electrotachyscope1.jpg/500px-Electrotachyscope1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Electrotachyscope1.jpg/500px-Electrotachyscope1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&